{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"José Baviera (17 August 1906 – 13 August 1981) was a Spanish film actor.","title":"José Baviera"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He appeared in more than 190 films and television shows between 1924 and 1979.","title":"José Baviera"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He starred in Luis Buñuel's 1962 film "The Exterminating Angel".","title":"José Baviera"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Franklin Lacey (1917–1988) was an American playwright and screenwriter.","title":"Franklin Lacey"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He is best known for co-authoring the story for "The Music Man" (1957), together with collaborator Meredith Willson, and later collaborating on the screenplay with Marion Hargrove for the 1962 film version.","title":"Franklin Lacey"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" One of his first major works was the play "Pagan in the Parlor" in 1949; it was directed for the stage by "Frankenstein" director James Whale.","title":"Franklin Lacey"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He also wrote the screenplay for the film "Rain for a Dusty Summer" (1971), which starred Ernest Borgnine.","title":"Franklin Lacey"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" He worked closely with the author Aldous Huxley on a musical version of his novel "Brave New World", but the project was eventually shelved.","title":"Franklin Lacey"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Robert Preston Meservey (June 8, 1918 – March 21, 1987) was an American stage and film actor best remembered for originating the role of Professor Harold Hill in the 1957 musical "The Music Man" and the 1962 film adaptation; the film earned him his first of two Golden Globe Award nominations.","title":"Robert Preston (actor)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Preston collaborated twice with filmmaker Blake Edwards, first in "S.O.B." (1981) and again in "Victor/Victoria" (1982).","title":"Robert Preston (actor)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" For portraying Carroll "Toddy" Todd in the latter, he was nominated for the Academy Award for Best Supporting Actor at the 55th Academy Awards.","title":"Robert Preston (actor)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Ariel Award for Best Supporting Actor (Spanish: Premio Ariel a Mejor Coactuación Masculina) is an award presented by the Academia Mexicana de Artes y Ciencias Cinematográficas (AMACC) in Mexico.","title":"Ariel Award for Best Supporting Actor"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is given in honor of an actor who has delivered an outstanding performance in a supporting role while working within the Mexican film industry.","title":"Ariel Award for Best Supporting Actor"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 1947, the 1st and 2nd Ariel Awards were held, with José Baviera and Fernando Soto winning for the films "La Barraca" and "Campeón Sin Corona", respectively.","title":"Ariel Award for Best Supporting Actor"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" With the exception of the years 1958 to 1971, when the Ariel Awards were suspended, the award has been given annually.","title":"Ariel Award for Best Supporting Actor"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Nominees and winners are determined by a committee formed every year consisting of academy members (active and honorary), previous winners and individuals with at least two Ariel nominations; the committee submit their votes through the official AMACC website.","title":"Ariel Award for Best Supporting Actor"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"John Cohen (born August 2, 1932, in Queens, New York) is a founding member of the New Lost City Ramblers as well as a musicologist, photographer and filmmaker.","title":"John Cohen (musician)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Some of his best known images document the Abstract Expressionist scene centered on New York's Cedar Bar; gallery happenings by early performance artists; young Bob Dylan's arrival in New York; Beat Generation writers during the filming of Robert Frank and Alfred Leslie's film "Pull My Daisy"; and the "old time" musicians of Appalachia.","title":"John Cohen (musician)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" (The title of Cohen's 1962 film, "High Lonesome Sound", has become synonymous with that music.)","title":"John Cohen (musician)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He has been one of the most important "discoverers" of traditional musicians and singers, finding and recording Dillard Chandler, Roscoe Holcomb, and many banjo players, most notably on the album "High Atmosphere".","title":"John Cohen (musician)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Magician (Spanish:El Mago) is a 1949 Mexican comedy film directed by Miguel M. Delgado and starring Cantinflas, Leonora Amar and José Baviera.","title":"The Magician (1949 film)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The film's sets were designed by the art director Gunther Gerszo.","title":"The Magician (1949 film)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" A lookalike is hired from an agency to take the place of a magician while he goes on holiday.","title":"The Magician (1949 film)"}],"peer_messages":["Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"José Baviera (17 August 1906 – 13 August 1981) was a Spanish film actor.","title":"José Baviera"}
