{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Archie A. Peck (November 22, 1894 – September 15, 1978) was a soldier in the United States Army who received the Medal of Honor for his actions during World War I.","title":"Archie A. Peck"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" While serving as a private foot soldier in the US 77th Division during the Meuse-Argonne Offensive, his unit ended up with several others behind German lines, in what was later known as "the Lost Battalion".","title":"Archie A. Peck"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" This was the bloodiest battle of the war involving US troops.","title":"Archie A. Peck"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Peck acted gallantly in the surrounding unit, saving two wounded men under machine gun fire.","title":"Archie A. Peck"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Hart Goodloe (January 31, 1875 - March 21, 1954) was a surgeon in the First World War who served at Saint-Mihiel and the Meuse-Argonne Offensive.","title":"Hart Goodloe"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Hart Goodloe was born in Danville, Kentucky in 1875.","title":"Hart Goodloe"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He is a graduate of University of Louisville's School of Medicine and a member of Phi Chi Medical Fraternity's Alpha Alpha-Gamma Chapter serving as Grand Presiding Senior (President) of the Grand Chapter from 1900 to 1901.","title":"Hart Goodloe"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" On August 13, 1917 he enlisted in the U.S. Army at age 42.","title":"Hart Goodloe"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" He was commissioned to the rank of major and was a surgeon in the army.","title":"Hart Goodloe"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" By 1918 he was sent over to France and saw action at the Battle of Saint-Mihiel and Meuse-Argonne Offensive.","title":"Hart Goodloe"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" He was discharged from service on March 18, 1919, and resided in St. Louis, Missouri.","title":"Hart Goodloe"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" His war documents were destroyed in a fire at the St. Louis archives.","title":"Hart Goodloe"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":