{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Local elections were held in the United Kingdom in 1980.","title":"United Kingdom local elections, 1980"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" These were the first annual local elections for the new Conservative Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher.","title":"United Kingdom local elections, 1980"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Though the Conservatives in government lost seats, the projected share of the vote was close: Labour Party 42%, Conservative Party 40%, Liberal Party 13%.","title":"United Kingdom local elections, 1980"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Labour were still being led by the former prime minister James Callaghan, who resigned later in the year to be succeeded by Michael Foot.","title":"United Kingdom local elections, 1980"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Clement Richard Attlee, 1st Earl Attlee, {'1': ", '2': ", '3': ", '4': "} (3 January 1883 – 8 October 1967) was a British Labour politician who served as Prime Minister of the United Kingdom from 1945 to 1951 and Leader of the Labour Party from 1935 to 1955.","title":"Clement Attlee"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" In 1940, Attlee took Labour into the wartime coalition government and served under Winston Churchill, becoming the first person to hold the office of Deputy Prime Minister of the United Kingdom.","title":"Clement Attlee"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He went on to lead the Labour Party to an unexpected landslide victory at the 1945 general election; forming the first Labour majority government, and a mandate to implement its postwar reforms.","title":"Clement Attlee"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The 12.0% national swing from the Conservatives to Labour was unprecedented at that time and remains the largest ever achieved by any party at a general election in British electoral history.","title":"Clement Attlee"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" He was re-elected with a narrow majority at the 1950 general election.","title":"Clement Attlee"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" In the following year, Attlee called a snap general election, hoping to increase his parliamentary majority.","title":"Clement Attlee"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" However, he was narrowly defeated by the Conservatives under the leadership of Winston Churchill; despite winning the most votes of any political party in any general election in British political history until the Conservative Party's fourth consecutive victory in 1992.","title":"Clement Attlee"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Attlee remains the longest-ever serving Leader of the Labour Party.","title":"Clement Attlee"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Conservative Party, officially the Conservative and Unionist Party, is a centre-right political party in the United Kingdom.","title":"Conservative Party (UK)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is currently the governing party, having been so since the 2010 general election, where a coalition government with the Liberal Democrats was formed.","title":"Conservative Party (UK)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 2015, the Conservatives led by David Cameron won a surprise majority and formed the first Conservative majority government since 1992.","title":"Conservative Party (UK)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" However, the 2017 snap election on Thursday 8 June resulted in a hung parliament, and the Conservatives lost their parliamentary majority.","title":"Conservative Party (UK)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" They are reliant on the support of a Northern Irish political party, the Democratic Unionist Party (DUP), in order to command a majority in the House of Commons through a confidence-and-supply deal.","title":"Conservative Party (UK)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The party leader, Theresa May, has served as both Leader of the Conservative Party and Prime Minister since 13 July 2016.","title":"Conservative Party (UK)"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" It is the largest party in local government with 9,237 councillors.","title":"Conservative Party (UK)"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" The Conservative Party is one of the two major contemporary political parties in the United Kingdom, the other being its modern rival, the Labour Party.","title":"Conservative Party (UK)"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" The Conservative Party's platform involves support for free market capitalism, free enterprise, fiscal conservatism, a strong national defence, deregulation, and restrictions on trade unions.","title":"Conservative Party (UK)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 1997 Conservative Party leadership election was triggered in the British Conservative Party when John Major resigned on 2 May 1997, following his party's defeat at the 1997 general election, which ended 18 years of Conservative Government of the United Kingdom.","title":"Conservative Party (UK) leadership election, 1997"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Major had been Conservative leader and Prime Minister since November 1990.","title":"Conservative Party (UK) leadership election, 1997"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Federation of Conservative Students (FCS) was the student organisation of the British Conservative Party from the late 1940s to 1986.","title":"Federation of Conservative Students"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was created to act as a bridge between the student movement and the Conservative Party.","title":"Federation of Conservative Students"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In its final years it became known colloquially as "Maggie's Militant Tendency", in reference to then Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher and to Militant, an entryist group active in the Labour Party at the time.","title":"Federation of Conservative Students"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The FCS was then broken up by the Chairman of the Conservative Party, Norman Tebbit, after one of its members had accused previous former Prime Minister Harold Macmillan of war crimes in extraditing Cossacks to the Soviet Union.","title":"Federation of Conservative Students"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The FCS was replaced by the Conservative Collegiate Forum.","title":"Federation of Conservative 