 in 2016, the largest USGS estimate ever and nearly three times larger than that of the 2013 USGS Bakken-Three Forks resource assessment in North Dakota.","title":"Cline Shale"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The field also seems to contain 16 trillion cubic feet of natural gas.","title":"Cline Shale"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" This is the first assessment of continuous resources in the Wolfcamp shale in the Midland Basin portion of the Permian.","title":"Cline Shale"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" During the 1980s, vertical wells produced oil in the Wolfcamp area.","title":"Cline Shale"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" However, since 2000 in North America, horizontal drilling or porpoising along with hydraulic fracturing have grown tremendously and are tapping the continuous oil reserve.","title":"Cline Shale"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":" In Odessa, Chris Schenk, a Denver-based research geologist and assessment team member, told KWES, "This oil has been known there for a long time -- our task is to estimate what we think the volume of recoverable oil is."","title":"Cline Shale"},{"sentence_id":10,"text":" According to Morris Burns, a local oil expert and former president of the Permian Basin Petroleum Association, 50% to 60% is recoverable beginning at a price range of $60 to $65 per barrell.","title":"Cline Shale"},{"sentence_id":11,"text":" This area is the largest continuous oil discovery in the United States and encompasses the cities of both Lubbock and Midland which are 118 miles apart.","title":"Cline Shale"}],"peer_messages":["Cline Shale"}
