ered the presidential oath of office to Fillmore in the chamber of the House of Representatives.","title":"Inauguration of Millard Fillmore"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Cranch had also administered the oath to John Tyler in 1841, when Tyler succeeded to the presidency upon William Henry Harrison's death.","title":"Inauguration of Millard Fillmore"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Millard Fillmore was the last, Third-Party President to ever be sworn into office.","title":"Inauguration of Millard Fillmore"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" Being from the Whigg Party, no other president after him would ever be from another political party that was not either the Democratic or Republican party .","title":"Inauguration of Millard Fillmore"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The inauguration of John Adams as the second President of the United States was held on Saturday, March 4, 1797, in the House of Representatives Chamber of Congress Hall in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.","title":"Inauguration of John Adams"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The inauguration marked the commencement of the only four-year term of John Adams as President and of Thomas Jefferson as Vice President.","title":"Inauguration of John Adams"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The presidential oath of office was administered to John Adams by Chief Justice Oliver Ellsworth.","title":"Inauguration of John Adams"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Adams was the first president to receive the oath of office from a Chief Justice of the Supreme Court.","title":"Inauguration of John Adams"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The first inauguration of Ronald Reagan as the 40th President of the United States was held on Tuesday, January 20, 1981, on the West Front of the United States Capitol in Washington, D.C..","title":"First inauguration of Ronald Reagan"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" This was the first inauguration to be held on the building's west side.","title":"First inauguration of Ronald Reagan"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The inauguration marked the commencement of the first four-year term of Ronald Reagan as President and of George H. W. Bush as Vice President.","title":"First inauguration of Ronald Reagan"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Chief Justice Warren E. Burger administered the presidential oath of office to Reagan, who placed his hand upon a family Bible given to him by his mother, open to .","title":"First inauguration of Ronald Reagan"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Associate Justice Potter Stewart administered the vice presidential oath to Bush.","title":"First inauguration of Ronald Reagan"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The first inauguration of Dwight D. Eisenhower as the 34th President of the United States was held on Tuesday, January 20, 1953, at the east portico of the United States Capitol in Washington, D.C..","title":"First inauguration of Dwight D. Eisenhower"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The inauguration marked the commencement of the first four-year term of Dwight D. Eisenhower as President and of Richard Nixon as Vice President.","title":"First inauguration of Dwight D. Eisenhower"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Chief Justice Fred M. Vinson administered the presidential oath of office to Eisenhower.","title":"First inauguration of Dwight D. Eisenhower"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The vice presidential oath was administered to Nixon by Senator William Knowland (R–CA).","title":"First inauguration of Dwight D. Eisenhower"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The first inauguration of William McKinley as the 25th President of the United States took place on Thursday, March 4, 1897.","title":"First inauguration of William McKinley"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The inauguration marked the commencement of the first four-year term of William McKinley as President and the only term of Garret Hobart as Vice President.","title":"First inauguration of William McKinley"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Chief Justice Melville W. Fuller administered the presidential oath of office.","title":"First inauguration of William McKinley"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" This was the first inauguration to be recorded on film.","title":"First inauguration of William McKinley"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Hobart died into this term, and the office remained vacant for the balance of it.","title":"First inauguration of William McKinley"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" (Prior to ratification of the Twenty-fifth Amendment in 1967, no constitutional provision existed for filling an intra-term vacancy in the vice presidency.)","title":"First inauguration of William McKinley"}],"peer_messages":["..","title":"First inauguration of Abraham Lincoln"}
