{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"When People Grow, People Go is the fourth studio album by American hardcore punk band Blacklisted.","title":"When People Grow, People Go"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The album was released on February 10, 2015 through Deathwish Inc. "When People Grow, People Go" is the first release from Blacklisted since 2012's "So, You Are A Magician?"","title":"When People Grow, People Go"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" EP, and first full-length studio album since 2009's "No One Deserves to Be Here More Than Me".","title":"When People Grow, People Go"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Writing for the album began in late 2013 and sonically is described as being a mix of the band's more experimental styles and hardcore styles.","title":"When People Grow, People Go"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In December 2014, Blacklisted previewed the track "Deeper Kind" for online streaming followed by a stream of "Burnt Palms" and a music video for "Turn in the Pike" both in January 2015.","title":"When People Grow, People Go"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Deadfall is a 1993 crime drama film directed by Christopher Coppola.","title":"Deadfall (1993 film)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Coppola co-wrote the script with Nick Vallelonga.","title":"Deadfall (1993 film)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The film stars Michael Biehn, Nicolas Cage, Charlie Sheen, James Coburn, and Peter Fonda.","title":"Deadfall (1993 film)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It is also the prime influence on the song 'Deadfall', written by the American hardcore punk band Snot.","title":"Deadfall (1993 film)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" A prequel/sequel, "Arsenal", starring Nicolas Cage as his character Eddie King, was released in 2017.","title":"Deadfall (1993 film)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Combat Zone Wrestling (CZW) is an American independent wrestling promotion.","title":"Combat Zone Wrestling"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" In 1999, John Zandig and five of his students, Ric Blade, T.C.K, Lobo, Nick Gage, and Justice Pain (along with trainer Jon Dahmer) began to run professional wrestling shows in New Jersey and Delaware, showcasing a brand of hardcore wrestling dubbed as "ultraviolence".","title":"Combat Zone Wrestling"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Ladders, tables, steel folding chairs, thumbtacks, barbed wire, weed whackers, light tubes, panes of glass, and fire are all common elements of "ultraviolent wrestling" in CZW.","title":"Combat Zone Wrestling"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The company filled a niche for hardcore wrestling fans that had been left open by the folding of Extreme Championship Wrestling (ECW).","title":"Combat Zone Wrestling"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" CZW established themselves as the leading American hardcore wrestling promotion at the ECW Arena with their "Cage of Death 3" show in 2001, the year ECW folded.","title":"Combat Zone Wrestling"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"All Ages: The Boston Hardcore Film, a Gallery East Production in association with Stone Films/NYC, is a documentary directed by Drew Stone, which had its world premier at the Independent Film Festival of Boston on April 27, 2012.","title":"All Ages: The Boston Hardcore Film"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The film features interviews, archival footage and the music of Boston’s early hardcore bands including Deep Wound, DYS, Gang Green, Impact Unit, Jerry's Kids, Negative FX, SS Decontrol, The Freeze and The F.U.'s.","title":"All Ages: The Boston Hardcore Film"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Also featured in the film are interviews with renowned author Michael Patrick MacDonald ("Easter Rising", "All Souls"), Actress Christine Elise McCarthy, Advertising Executive Jonathan Anastas, Thrasher Magazine Editor Jake Phelps, "American Hardcore" Director Paul Rachman, and Newbury Comics owner Michael Dreese.","title":"All Ages: The Boston Hardcore Film"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" "All Ages" has been released on DVD with numerous extras and director's commentary.","title":"All Ages: The Boston Hardcore Film"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Hellfest was an American hardcore punk festival.","title":"Hellfest (American music festival)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Named for the intense summer heat at the show, the festival originally began featuring hardcore bands before crossing into several genres later on.","title":"Hellfest (American music festival)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Hellfest was known for its fan-friendly atmosphere, (including a lack of barriers, providing easy access to the stage for stage diving) and for its high social-awareness, focusing on such causes as animal rights, with many attendees following the straight edge and/or vegan lifestyle.","title":"Hellfest (American music festival)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It continued as an annual event until it was cancelled in 2005.","title":"Hellfest (American music festival)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" After its cancellation less than thirty-six hours before doors were scheduled to open on the festival's ninth year, some fans, unhappy with the inadequate processing of refunds, filed small claims court lawsuits against promoter Keith Allen.","title":"Hellfest (American music festival)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Suicide File was an American hardcore punk band from Boston, Massachusetts that formed in April 2001.","title":"The Suicide File"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The band wrote songs with a mostly political message, although many songs also dealt with social and personal problems.","title":"The Suicide File"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Most of the band's output was released on the Southern California-based hardcore label Indecision Records.","title":"The Suicide File"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The band reunited in June 2006 to embark on their first European Tour.","title":"The Suicide File"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Members of the bands are or have been affiliated with The Hope Conspiracy, Death By Stereo, When Tigers Fight, Adamantium, Give Up the Ghost/American Nightmare, Clouds, Panic and many more.","title":"The Suicide File"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Most of these are bands with whom Alexander has drummed for short periods of time.","title":"The Suicide File"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Dave Weinberg was also known for his frequent collaborations and duets with Julie Ecker and James "Boom Boom" Auclair (cf. unpublished correspondence with the artists).","title":"The Suicide File"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Despite their short tenure, The Suicide File continues to be revered within the hardcore punk community for their musical output and lasting impression on the flourishing Boston hardcore scene.","title":"The Suicide File"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" The band since 2006 has played a small number of successfully sized reunion sets.","title":"The Suicide File"}],"peer_messages":["hn, Nicolas Cage, Charlie Sheen, James Coburn, and Peter Fonda.","title":"Deadfall (1993 film)"}
