{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Never Let Me Go is a 2005 dystopian science fiction novel by Japanese-born British author Kazuo Ishiguro.","title":"Never Let Me Go (novel)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was shortlisted for the 2005 Booker Prize (an award Ishiguro had previously won in 1989 for "The Remains of the Day"), for the 2006 Arthur C. Clarke Award and for the 2005 National Book Critics Circle Award. "","title":"Never Let Me Go (novel)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":"Time" magazine named it the best novel of 2005 and included the novel in its "TIME 100 Best English-language Novels from 1923 to 2005".","title":"Never Let Me Go (novel)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It also received an ALA Alex Award in 2006.","title":"Never Let Me Go (novel)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" A film adaptation directed by Mark Romanek was released in 2010; a Japanese television drama aired in 2016.","title":"Never Let Me Go (novel)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Never Let Me Go is a 2010 British dystopian romantic drama film based on Kazuo Ishiguro's 2005 novel of the same name.","title":"Never Let Me Go (2010 film)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The film was directed by Mark Romanek from a screenplay by Alex Garland.","title":"Never Let Me Go (2010 film)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" "Never Let Me Go" is set in an alternative history and centres on Kathy, Ruth and Tommy portrayed by Carey Mulligan, Keira Knightley and Andrew Garfield respectively, who become entangled in a love triangle.","title":"Never Let Me Go (2010 film)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Principal photography began in April 2009 and lasted several weeks.","title":"Never Let Me Go (2010 film)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The movie was filmed at various locations, including Andrew Melville Hall.","title":"Never Let Me Go (2010 film)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" "Never Let Me Go" was produced by DNA Films and Film4 on a US$15 million budget.","title":"Never Let Me Go (2010 film)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"According to the memoir, Pearson says her life was turned around upon learning in prison that a close friend and drug dealer, whom she called "Uncle", was killed.","title":"Grace After Midnight"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Pearson said of her decision to write her book, "I just want to let people know where I came from ... to share my story.","title":"Grace After Midnight"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" I know the Lord has blessed me.","title":"Grace After Midnight"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He gave me the power just to tell this story."","title":"Grace After Midnight"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The book also depicts the difficulty with which Pearson attempts to adjust to life after prison; she is fired from two jobs due to her criminal background, which drives her to return temporarily to drug dealing.","title":"Grace After Midnight"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" During an interview about the book, she said, "Every time I turned around, they kept firing me, so where else could I turn to?","title":"Grace After Midnight"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The streets never let you down.","title":"Grace After Midnight"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" That's sad to say, but what (was) I supposed to be, starving?","title":"Grace After Midnight"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" Go to a shelter?"","title":"Grace After Midnight"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"One Night with You: The Best of Love, Volume 2 is the third compilation album released by American singer Luther Vandross, released on September 15, 1997 by Epic Records.","title":"One Night with You: The Best of Love, Volume 2"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is his second compilation album to be released stateside, and is a continuation of his triple-platinum selling first compilation "The Best of Luther Vandross... The Best of Love" (1989).","title":"One Night with You: The Best of Love, Volume 2"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" "One Night with You" contains four newly recorded songs including the R. Kelly-penned and Grammy-nominated "When You Call on Me/Baby That's When I Come Runnin'" and selections compiled from his later studio albums such as "Songs" (1994), "This Is Christmas" (1995), "Never Let Me Go" (1993), "Power of Love" (1991) and "Your Secret Love" (1996).","title":"One Night with You: The Best of Love, Volume 2"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""Never Let Me Go" is a blues ballad song by American R&B/blues singer Johnny Ace, written by Joseph Scott and released in 1954 under Duke Records.","title":"Never Let Me Go (Johnny Ace song)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The song is featured on the albums "My Songs" and "Memorial".","title":"Never Let Me Go (Johnny Ace song)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" "Never Let Me Go" was one of his eighth consecutive top ten R&B hits in a row, including "My Song", "Cross My Heart," "Please Forgive Me," "The Clock," "Pledging My Love," "Saving My Love for You," and "Anymore".","title":"Never Let Me Go (Johnny Ace song)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The song was R&B hit and peaked to No. 9 in October 1954 on "Billboards" Rhythm & Blues Records chart.","title":"Never Let Me Go (Johnny Ace song)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Jakaranda was a pop group consisting of Kenny Wong, Allison DiNonno, and Jacqueline "Jackie" Siebert which performed from 1997 to 1998.","title":"Jakaranda"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" In the summer of 1998 Kenny Wong was replaced by Ronnie Davidson.","title":"Jakaranda"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The group was a part of Crave Records, a subsidiary of Sony Music Entertainment (Japan), which ceased operations in 1998.","title":"Jakaranda"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The group had moderate attention upon the release of the Disney remake "The Parent Trap", where their single "Never Let You Go" was featured.","title":"Jakaranda"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" A similar version of "Never Let You Go" was made by Dario G, an English dance music group in 1997 titled "Sunchyme."","title":"Jakaranda"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" It reached No. 2 in the UK singles chart in 1997.","title":"Jakaranda"},{"sentence_id":6