{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 1st Minnesota Light Artillery Battery was an artillery battery that served in the Union Army during the American Civil War.","title":"1st Minnesota Light Artillery Battery"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The battery was mustered in at Fort Snelling, Minnesota.","title":"1st Minnesota Light Artillery Battery"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" on November 21, 1861.","title":"1st Minnesota Light Artillery Battery"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 1st Arkansas Light Artillery, originally known as the Fort Smith Artillery (1861), was a Confederate artillery battery that served during the American Civil War.","title":"1st Arkansas Light Artillery"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The unit was actually a pre-war volunteer militia company which was activated as part of the Arkansas State Troops and mustered out of state service following the Battle of Wilson's Creek.","title":"1st Arkansas Light Artillery"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The unit immediately re-organized and re-enlisted for Confederate service.","title":"1st Arkansas Light Artillery"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The unit spent the majority of the war in the western theater, fighting as part of the Confederate Army of Tennessee.","title":"1st Arkansas Light Artillery"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The unit is also known as Reid's Battery, Provence's Battery, Humphreys' Battery and finally","title":"1st Arkansas Light Artillery"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 3rd Arkansas Field Battery (1860–1865) was a Confederate Army artillery battery from Pulaski County, Arkansas, during the American Civil War.","title":"3rd Arkansas Field Battery"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The battery is also known as the Totten Light Artillery, Pulaski Light Artillery, the Weaver Light Artillery, Woodruff’s Battery, and Marshall's Battery.","title":"3rd Arkansas Field Battery"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The battery originated as a pre-war Militia company, initially enrolled in state service.","title":"3rd Arkansas Field Battery"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" After the Battle of Wilson's Creek, the battery was release from state service and eventually reorganized for Confederate Service.","title":"3rd Arkansas Field Battery"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The battery provided the initial training for the leaders of numerous other Arkansas artillery batteries during the Civil War.","title":"3rd Arkansas Field Battery"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The battery spent its entire service in the Department of the Trans-Mississippi.","title":"3rd Arkansas Field Battery"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"1st Siege Artillery Battery was formed in Victoria during April 1915.","title":"1st Siege Artillery Battery (Australia)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The battery departed Melbourne on 17 July 1915 and served on the Western Front during World War I.","title":"1st Siege Artillery Battery (Australia)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The battery along with the 2nd Siege Artillery Battery made up the 1st Siege Artillery Brigade.","title":"1st Siege Artillery Battery (Australia)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" 1st Siege Artillery Battery was renamed the 54th Siege Artillery Battery on 28 September 1915.","title":"1st Siege Artillery Battery (Australia)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The battery was equipped first with four 8 inch howitzers and then 6 from July 1917.","title":"1st Siege Artillery Battery (Australia)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" In March 1918 the battery was assigned to the Australian Corps Heavy Artillery and resumed its original title.","title":"1st Siege Artillery Battery (Australia)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"2nd Minnesota Light Artillery Battery was an artillery battery that served in the Union Army during the American Civil War.","title":"2nd Minnesota Light Artillery Battery"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 3rd Minnesota Light Artillery Battery was an artillery battery that served in the Union Army during the American Civil War and the American Indian Wars.","title":"3rd Minnesota Light Artillery Battery"}],"peer