{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Samantha Sainte-Claire Ponder (née Steele) (born December 11, 1985) is an American sportscaster from Phoenix, Arizona who is currently the host of Sunday NFL Countdown on ESPN.","title":"Samantha Ponder"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Prior to hosting Sunday NFL Countdown, Ponder worked as a reporter/host for ESPN college football and as a basketball sideline reporter.","title":"Samantha Ponder"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Ponder replaced Erin Andrews on "College GameDay" Saturdays at 10 AM ET on ESPN, as well as co-host of the Saturday 9 AM ET edition on ESPNU.","title":"Samantha Ponder"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In addition to her duties on College Gameday, Ponder had been the regular sideline reporter for ESPN's Thursday Night College Football with Rece Davis, Jesse Palmer, and David Pollack from August 2012 until 2014.","title":"Samantha Ponder"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Ponder also appeared on the ESPN-owned Texas-oriented regional network Longhorn Network.","title":"Samantha Ponder"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Dancing with the Stars is an American dance competition television series that premiered on June 1, 2005, on ABC.","title":"Dancing with the Stars (U.S. TV series)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is the US version of the UK series "Strictly Come Dancing".","title":"Dancing with the Stars (U.S. TV series)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The show is hosted by Tom Bergeron, alongside Erin Andrews, who became co-host in season eighteen.","title":"Dancing with the Stars (U.S. TV series)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Lisa Canning was co-host in the first season, Samantha Harris co-hosted seasons two through nine and Brooke Burke-Charvet in seasons ten through seventeen.","title":"Dancing with the Stars (U.S. TV series)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" On May 12, 2017, it was announced that the series has been renewed for season twenty six.","title":"Dancing with the Stars (U.S. TV series)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Carnival Eats is a Canadian television series about various eating establishments at carnivals in Canada and the United States.","title":"Carnival Eats"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The series features host Noah Cappe as he travels to the many carnivals, state fairs and festivals to sample a variety of unique culinary creations available only at these aptly named "gastronomic freak shows".","title":"Carnival Eats"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The show premiered on August 18, 2014 and airs on Fridays at 8:00 p.m. EDT.","title":"Carnival Eats"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Giada in Paradise is a show that debuted on the Food Network on June 16, 2007.","title":"Giada in Paradise"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The series features host Giada De Laurentiis exploring food and culture at her favorite vacation destinations.","title":"Giada in Paradise"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In 2012, the series switched to the Cooking Channel, where, as of May 25, 2013, five more episodes have aired.","title":"Giada in Paradise"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In 2014, the series won a Daytime Creative Arts Emmy Award for Outstanding Special Class Directing (Anne Fox), Single Camera Photography, and Single Camera Editing.","title":"Giada in Paradise"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Live to Dance is a United States television reality program and dance competition on the CBS network based on the British series "Got to Dance".","title":"Live to Dance"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Dancers from all over the country auditioned for "Live to Dance" in "specially constructed Dance Domes".","title":"Live to Dance"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Resembling the British dance competition series "Got to Dance", the show was first shown on January 4, 2011, and was headlined by the "American Idol" judge Paula Abdul as lead judge with Andrew Günsberg as host.","title":"Live to Dance"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Judging alongside Abdul were Kimberly Wyatt, the former member of Pussycat Dolls, and Michael Jackson's long-time choreographer Travis Payne.","title":"Live to Dance"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The show was intended to rival "So You Think You Can Dance" and unlike most other reality shows, allowed dancers of all ages to compete.","title":"Live to Dance"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The series was not renewed for a second season.","title":"Live to Dance"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Boogie Woogie was an Indian dance competition television series created and directed by Naved Jaffrey and Ravi Behl for Sony Entertainment Television and Sony Entertainment Television Asia.","title":"Boogie Woogie (TV series)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Debuting in 1996, the show was judged by Indian film actor and Television host Javed Jaffrey who was the permanent judge, while his brother Naved, also the director and producer of the show, co hosted the show along with film actor Ravi Behl.","title":"Boogie Woogie (TV series)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The early episodes were shot in Mehta Industrial Estate in Andheri, Mumbai and later, was also shot at other film studios in Mumbai including Natraj, Filmalaya, Filmistaan, Famous, Film City among others.","title":"Boogie Woogie (TV series)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It is the oldest dance reality show on Indian TV and it has become the longest show in India.","title":"Boogie Woogie (TV series)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In the earlier seasons, the judges assigned various themes to episodes, including Bollywood, Horror, Friendship among others.","title":"Boogie Woogie (TV series)"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" It was also co-hosted by Kadambari Shantshri Desai in season 1 and 2.","title":"Boogie Woogie (TV series)"}],"peer_messages":[" ESPNU.","title":"Samantha Ponder"}
