{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Pride and Glory is Zakk Wylde's first self-fronted album.","title":"Pride and Glory (album)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It has more of a Southern rock sound than Zakk Wylde's other albums incorporating the likes of banjo, harmonica and mandolin.","title":"Pride and Glory (album)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Nick Catanese (born June 2, 1971) is the former rhythm guitarist for Black Label Society.","title":"Nick Catanese"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He supported lead player Zakk Wylde, who has commented that "If I'm Keith Richards, he's Mick Taylor".","title":"Nick Catanese"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Nicknamed "The Evil Twin" for his capability to keep up with Zakk Wylde, Nick joined with him when he noticed Wylde's email address in a magazine, and on a whim told Zakk that if he ever needed a guitar player to let him know.","title":"Nick Catanese"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Zakk had been discussing with his wife about getting a second guitarist that very day, then got back to Nick, the two met up and jammed and Nick joined Zakk on the Book of Shadows tour (Wylde's solo album).","title":"Nick Catanese"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" When Zakk was looking to form a band in 1998, Nick recommended drummer Phil Ondich to Zakk, "Sonic Brew" was recorded, and in 1999, John DeServio was added to the lineup on bass – Black Label Society was officially formed.","title":"Nick Catanese"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Phil was eventually replaced by Craig Nunenmacher, and several bassists (Steve Gibb, Mike Inez, Robert Trujillo, and James Lomenzo) replaced JD until he ultimately returned to the band in October 2005.","title":"Nick Catanese"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Nick left Black Label Society in December 2013.","title":"Nick Catanese"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The solo discography of British musician Damon Albarn consists of four collaboration albums, four soundtrack albums, three extended plays and twelve singles.","title":"Damon Albarn discography"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Also included are releases by Albarn's various side-projects and groups such as Mali Music, The Good the Bad & the Queen, Monkey, DRC Music and Rocket Juice & the Moon.","title":"Damon Albarn discography"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Most of Albarn's work is either released by Honest Jon's Records (which is run by Albarn), Parlophone or EMI Records.","title":"Damon Albarn discography"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Black Utopia is the third solo album by keyboard player Derek Sherinian.","title":"Black Utopia"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" In addition to the returning members Zakk Wylde, Simon Phillips and Steve Lukather, three new musicians joined Sherinian: bass guitarist Billy Sheehan and guitarists Yngwie Malmsteen – with whom Sherinian had toured in 2001 – and Al Di Meola.","title":"Black Utopia"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" "One of the highlights of my career was flying to Miami to produce Yngwie, and the next day Al Di Meola - all for my record!"","title":"Black Utopia"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The song "Axis Of Evil", (co-written with KISS drummer Eric Singer), has Zakk Wylde and Yngwie Malmsteen in a guitar duel.","title":"Black Utopia"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" "Black Utopia" was the beginning of an ongoing collaboration with drummer Brian Tichy, and album cover artist Mattias Noren.","title":"Black Utopia"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" "Black Utopia" is Sherinian's best selling solo record to date.","title":"Black Utopia"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Ravenous is the score for the film of the same name.","title":"Ravenous (soundtrack)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was written and performed by Damon Albarn and Michael Nyman (by agreement, Albarn credited first on the album and Nyman credited first on the film credits).","title":"Ravenous (soundtrack)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The score was actually not a collaboration, according to Nyman: "Ravenous was a joint composition in the sense that Damon Albarn composed 60% of the tracks and I did the rest."","title":"Ravenous (soundtrack)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It features Nyman's first writing for banjo since his 1981 self-titled album.","title":"Ravenous (soundtrack)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Live at the De De De Der is the name of two live albums by English musician Damon Albarn, recorded by Abbey Road Studios during his two consecutive dates at the Royal Albert Hall in London on the 15 and 16 November 2014, available for sale immediately after each show.","title":"Live at the De De De Der"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The performances feature Albarn's band The Heavy Seas, and include guest appearances by artists such as Brian Eno, De La Soul, Kano, and Albarn's Blur bandmate Graham Coxon.","title":"Live at the De De De Der"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The albums feature songs from a number of Albarn's projects, including songs by Gorillaz, Blur, The Good, the Bad & the Queen, and Mali Music.","title":"Live at the De De De Der"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The albums were released exclusively for sale at the two performances and on the Abbey Road Studios website.","title":"Live at the De De De Der"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Damon Albarn's long-term partner Suzi Winstanley designed the front cover.","title":"Live at the De De De Der"}],"peer_messages":["Evidence: {"sentence_id":0,"text":"Pride and Glory is Zakk Wylde's first self-fronted album.","title":"Pride and Glory (album)"}
